{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"No. For an ordinary U.S. ’40 Act ETF, the portfolio is held for the fund—not owned by its adviser or sponsor. If the sponsor is a separate legal entity, its creditors generally cannot claim fund assets merely because the sponsor becomes insolvent, and sponsor insolvency by itself does not erase ETF shares.\n\nThat is **asset separation**, not a guarantee of an uninterrupted or loss-free outcome. The fund may still face operational disruption, replacement of the adviser, liquidation, trading halts, or losses from market movements, fraud, custody failures, or other legal and factual complications. If the fund itself becomes insolvent or is liquidated, shareholders’ recovery depends on the fund’s assets and liabilities; ETF shares are not insured against loss."}}
